Thanks Bertram,

I don´t think it is a good idea to remove the optimizations.  I think I might be able to make converted MDArrays immutable by undefining all the assignment methods.  Then, when converting with 'md' method, allow for a second argument to this method.  If it is true, then let the array be mutable.  In this way, by default, users will not be able to make any changes, but when knowing what they are doing, then they can make changes.  What do you think?

My concern is that having a very large array and for some reason needing to update a value, then be forced to make a whole copy of the very large array.  This could happen, for instance, if we get data updates from the internet.  But if users know what they are doing, them they can make sure that every operation in Renjin is done before doing the update.

Now a question... I´ve asked before but got no answer, but I think it is important:  When running SciCom inside JRuby shell (Windows, do know if the same happens on other platforms), trying to print anything does not generate any output. If I start JRuby inside a command shell (cmd) then everything works.  I´m presuming that the output of calling eval(print(x)) is not going straight to standard output.  Could this be the case?  If I call doPring(SEXP) and get a string, then I can print this string both in JRuby shell as in the cmd shell.  Is there another method similar to doPring that returns a string of print(x) instead of printing it?

Second question: would you be interested in writing a paper for UseR! or any other journal or conference on Renjin/SciCom?

Happy 2015!!

Em quarta-feira, 31 de dezembro de 2014 15h38min37s UTC-2, Alexander Bertram escreveu:
> Congrats Rodrigo -- Looks very exciting!
>
> Modifying the backing storage for a Renjin vector however may lead to quite some undefined behavior on Renjin's part however: Renjin will do it's best to defer operations on large matrices/vectors as long as possible in order to avoid allocating memory for intermediate results, and to apply optimizations like loop fusioning that are only possible if you have a large number of operations to optimize.
>
> So in the following example, you may not get what you expect:
>
> x <- hugeExternalMatrixFromSomewhere()
> y <- x      # y *2 contains now only a reference to x, nothing has been computed
> changeBackingStoreOfXtoSomethingCompletelyDifferent() # changes y as well !
>
> Maybe we can talk in the new year about your use cases to see how best to get Renjin to do what you would expect. I guess we could have some sort of "volatile" property of a vector that would prevent lazy evaluation and limit access to a vector to a single thread...? But then of course none of the optimizers will kick in....
>
> Happy new year!
>
>
>

